Israel and Morocco agreed on Wednesday to open embassies in each other’s countries following talks between their foreign ministers and the US ambassador to the United Nations in New York. The two countries established diplomatic ties in 2020 under the US-brokered Abraham Accords, which normalised Israel’s relations with several Arab states.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/89d8337a-b209-11f1-9497-757719c1152f/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23249705702543-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/89d8337a-b209-11f1-9497-757719c1152f/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23249705702543-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4e101552-b221-11f1-b75a-3be68007f0a3</guid><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 01:07:04 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/89d8337a-b209-11f1-9497-757719c1152f/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23249705702543-1.jpg">© Mosa'ab Elshamy, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>A propos</category><title>Ed Sheeran's tour in trouble after opening acts drop out in solidarity with rapper Macklemore</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20260916-ed-sheeran-s-tour-in-trouble-after-opening-acts-drop-out-in-solidarity-with-rapper-macklemore</link><description>
Ed Sheeran's tour of North and South America has been roiled by the decision this week to drop Macklemore as an opener because of the rapper's onstage pro-Palestinian comments. Since then, all of Sheeran's other supporting acts have dropped out, including a group that backs Sheeran up during part of the show. FRANCE 24's Monte Francis speaks with David Jackson, Professor of Political Science at Bowling Green State University about the issue.
